Issues #170 and #199 — the caller half: make the merge sweep prove it read all
of the pull request, and make it stop taking the head commit's word for when
that commit arrived.

ONE PATCH FOR ONE WORKFLOW, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A CONVENIENCE.
#199 needed four more edits to `pr-merge-sweep.yml` while #170's four were still
parked. Parking them separately would have produced two patches against one
file, an apply order to get right by hand, and a `merge-candidate.sh` arity that
moves twice — which is the state T-144 exists to stop growing, not to feed. So
they travel together: the live sweep passes nine arguments today and eleven the
moment this lands, with nothing in between for anybody to land halfway.

WHY THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A COMMIT. Agents here run as `claude[bot]` through
the Claude GitHub App, `ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN` is unset by design, and that
installation token holds no `workflows` permission. Verified on 2026-08-24 by
pushing a one-character change to a scratch branch:

    ! [remote rejected] probe/wf-push-170 -> probe/wf-push-170
      (refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow
       `.github/workflows/pr-merge-sweep.yml` without `workflows` permission)

So the rules, their filters, their query and their tests are on `main`, and the
eight edits that make the sweep USE them are here. Until this lands the sweep
merges nothing: `merge-candidate.sh` refuses a nine-argument caller by arity and
says so in the log, once per open pull request per sweep, naming this file. That
is the intended state -- a gate that cannot prove it read the whole pull request,
and cannot say when its head arrived, must not merge one -- but it is not the
finished one.

TO APPLY, from a session whose token may write workflows:

    git checkout main && git pull
    git apply docs/automation/pending/170-merge-sweep-completeness.patch
    git rm docs/automation/pending/170-merge-sweep-completeness.patch
    #  This file only. `git rm -r docs/automation/pending` would also delete
    #  `75-approval-stall.patch` -- 34 kB parked by #128, on `main` today and
    #  not yet applied -- the directory's README, and #154's patch when it
    #  lands. See T-144.
    bash .github/tests/merge-candidate-test.sh   # 182 assertions, and the last
                                                 # four flip to the applied state

THEN THE FIVE DOCUMENTS THAT DESCRIBE THE PARKED STATE, in the same commit.
This directory's README says a patch carries every edit its own landing forces,
and nothing tests prose: the suite checks that this file is gone, not that the
sentences about it are true. Applying the patch without these leaves five
documents asserting the opposite of the truth, which is the defect
CLAUDE_AUTOMATION.md calls blocking on #154, mirrored. The list was three for
three rounds, and the two it was missing were the README that states the rule
and the file CLAUDE.md sends every agent to first. Before committing, run all
four of `grep -rn 170-merge-sweep-completeness`, `grep -rn 'merges nothing'`,
`grep -rn 'HALF LIVE'` and `grep -rn 'pr-merge-sweep.yml' docs/`. Not one of
them is redundant, and each was added after the list came up short without it:
the conditions-table row above the banner contains neither of the first two
strings; `merges nothing` hits an unrelated undrafting row in the same file;
and the README paragraph that says THIS is the one patch editing the sweep
names neither the patch nor any of those phrases -- it says *this one*. Three
greps looked like they had covered the file three rounds running.

